Tool Usage, Dataset Analysis, and Proof of Concept (PoC)
Project Context

This project focuses on few-shot retinal disease classification using Prototypical Networks and pre-trained CNN models. Due to limited labeled medical data, we adopt a metric-based few-shot learning approach and evaluate its feasibility through partial implementation and exploratory data analysis (EDA).

Tools, Libraries, and Tutorials Used

Framework: PyTorch – for model implementation and training

Models: VGG19 and ResNet50 (pre-trained on ImageNet)

Dataset Handling: Pandas, NumPy

Image Processing: OpenCV, PIL

Visualization: Matplotlib, Seaborn

Dataset: RFMiD 2021 Retinal Fundus Image Dataset

Tutorials/References:

PyTorch Prototypical Networks tutorial

RFMiD Dataset documentation

Python Graph Gallery (for visualization techniques)

Dataset Overview and Raw Data Analysis

The RFMiD 2021 dataset contains 3200 retinal fundus images with 46 disease categories, accompanied by a CSV file storing image-level labels.

Basic Dataset Statistics (Raw Data):

Total images: 3200

Training set: 1920 images

Validation set: 640 images

Test set: 640 images

Image type: RGB fundus images

Resolution (varies): resized to 224×224 during preprocessing

Using Pandas, we analyzed the CSV label file to observe class distributions. The dataset is highly imbalanced, with some diseases having very few samples, validating the need for few-shot learning.

Data Cleaning and Preprocessing

Images were manually cropped and resized to 224×224

Label inconsistencies were checked using CSV inspection

Contrast enhancement applied using CLAHE

Data augmentation applied:

Horizontal & vertical flips

90° and 180° rotations

After cleaning, image visibility and feature clarity improved significantly.

Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)

Following standard EDA steps:

Class Frequency Bar Chart: showed severe class imbalance

Sample Image Visualization: common vs uncommon disease classes

Pixel Intensity Distribution Plot: confirmed normalization benefits

Two example visualizations were generated:

Bar plot of number of samples per disease class

Histogram of pixel intensity values before and after CLAHE

These analyses guided the selection of few-shot learning instead of conventional classification.

Proof of Concept (PoC): Partial Implementation

A basic PoC was implemented to validate feasibility.

Steps Implemented:

Feature extraction using VGG19 and ResNet50

Creation of N-way K-shot tasks (e.g., 2-way 10-shot, 3-way 5-shot)

Prototype computation by averaging support set embeddings

Classification of query images using Euclidean distance

Model optimization using cross-entropy loss

Sample Output:

Configuration: 2-way 10-shot classification

Model: VGG19

Accuracy: 80%

Loss: 0.364

This result demonstrates that the proposed approach can correctly classify unseen retinal disease classes with limited labeled samples.

Minor Configuration Changes from Online Code

Modified input pipeline to handle RFMiD fundus images

Replaced standard classifier head with prototype-based distance computation

Adjusted learning rate and freezing strategy for medical images

These changes made the implementation more suitable for retinal disease classification.

Conclusion

The tool usage, dataset analysis, and PoC results confirm that few-shot learning with prototypical networks is a promising approach for retinal disease classification under data scarcity. The partial implementation successfully demonstrates feasibility and provides a strong foundation for full-scale experimentation and optimization.
